/gradlew text eol=lf
# Deploy scripts run on the Linux host: a CRLF checkout breaks the shebang line.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.jar binary
# .env templates must be checked out with LF: a CRLF would put a trailing \r into every
# value. .env itself is never tracked, so this only guards the template.
deploy/docker/.env* text eol=lf
# The deploy tree is tar'd verbatim to a Linux host and parsed by bash/compose. With
# core.autocrlf=true (the Windows default here) every file without an eol rule comes back as
# CRLF on the next checkout, which silently appends \r to .env values and hides `${VAR:?…}`
# from deploy/bin/check-env.sh. Pin the whole tree instead of per-file.
deploy/** text eol=lf
